data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"data\": {\n    \"lookupStatus\": \"found\",\n    \"post\": {\n      \"platform\": \"facebook\",\n      \"postId\": \"1640678950582058\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/groups/1098058294844129/posts/1640678950582058/\",\n      \"description\": \"EDIT: I am overwhelmed with all of your supportive and encouraging replies. It's heartwarming to know complete strangers are here to help so willingly. I have read each and every comment and plan to reread, take notes, share with my son, and come up with a plan together using your suggestions. Hopefully my post will help others in the same situation. Thank you all so much! ❤️\\n\\nI feel like I've failed my son. We live in a small town. My son has played baseball his entire life. He has gotten academic and athletic honors for baseball in HS and had very good stats his junior year. It was the best season he's had. His HS years of playing have been a revolving door of coaches. He has not attended showcases, but has played on a PG team as well.\\n\\nHe is currently a senior. He has always had a dream of playing at the college level and is focusing on junior colleges in our state. I think its too late now. He has emailed JUCO coaches. No responses. He sent follow up emails. No responses. He made his own video and I don't think its probably the quality they're looking for, possibly? I feel like we're navigating the process blindly. I wasn't aware of the recruiting process and now I feel it's my fault he's in this position and he is getting frustrated.\\n\\nCan anyone give me advice on next steps? Is it too late and we accept and move on? Any advice is welcome. Thank you\",\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-03-14T14:30:27.000Z\",\n      \"isPaidPartnership\": null,\n      \"sharedPostId\": null,\n      \"sharedPostUrl\": null,\n      \"feedbackId\": \"ZmVlZGJhY2s6cGZiaWQweXoyMjlMUWhpdHhiWUJyOVpKYTRTVktjOVV0aXBIcDZzMm9VdXVtQmVlNlNtSjk1ZER0NHhtMm4xdGdqWUZuS2w=\",\n   